```js
const WMTS_CAPS = 'https://wmts.geo.admin.ch/EPSG/3857/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml';

/**
 * Parse the WMTS 1.0.0 GetCapabilities document into:
 *   layers: Map<layerId, {id,title,timeValues,urlTemplate,tileMatrixSet,maxZoom,extent}>
 *   matrixSets: Map<setId, maxZoom>
 * Only `<Layer>` blocks are considered; the TileMatrixSet definitions give the
 * highest usable zoom level (matrix identifiers are 0-based).
 */
function parseWmtsCapabilities(xml) {
  const matrixSets = new Map();
  for (const block of xml.split('<TileMatrixSet>').slice(1)) {
    const id = block.match(/<ows:Identifier>([^<]+)<\/ows:Identifier>/);
    if (!id) continue;
    const count = block.split('<TileMatrix>').length - 1;
    if (count > 0) matrixSets.set(id[1], count - 1);
  }
  const layers = new Map();
  for (const block of xml.split('<Layer>').slice(1)) {
    const id = block.match(/<ows:Identifier>([^<]+)<\/ows:Identifier>/);
    if (!id) continue;
    const title = block.match(/<ows:Title>([^<]*)<\/ows:Title>/);
    const dim = block.match(/<Dimension[^>]*>([\s\S]*?)<\/Dimension>/);
    const timeValues = dim ? [...dim[1].matchAll(/<Value>([^<]+)<\/Value>/g)].map((m) => decode(m[1])) : [];
    const template = block.match(/<ResourceURL[^>]*template="([^"]+)"/);
    const matrixSet = block.match(/<TileMatrixSetLink>[\s\S]*?<TileMatrixSet>([^<]+)<\/TileMatrixSet>/);
    const bbox = block.match(/<ows:WGS84BoundingBox>[\s\S]*?<ows:LowerCorner>([^<]+)<\/ows:LowerCorner>[\s\S]*?<ows:UpperCorner>([^<]+)<\/ows:UpperCorner>/);
    if (!timeValues.length || !template) continue;
    const setId = matrixSet ? matrixSet[1] : null;
    layers.set(id[1], {
      id: id[1],
      title: title ? decode(title[1]) : id[1],
      timeValues,
      urlTemplate: template[1],
      tileMatrixSet: setId,
      maxZoom: (setId && matrixSets.get(setId)) || 18,
      extent: bbox ? {...} : null,
    });
  }
  return { layers, matrixSets };
}
```

Wait, careful with splitting on `<TileMatrixSet>`: the document contains `<TileMatrixSetLink><TileMatrixSet>3857_18</TileMatrixSet></TileMatrixSetLink>` inside layer blocks, and the actual TileMatrixSet definitions at the end. Both contain the string `<TileMatrixSet>`. The definitions are `<TileMatrixSet>` elements; the link uses `<TileMatrixSet>` child element too. So `xml.split('<TileMatrixSet>')` will split at every link as well. Need to be careful: the definitions come after `<TileMatrixSet>` with `<ows:Identifier>` right after and contain `<TileMatrix>` children. The link blocks are `<TileMatrixSetLink>...<TileMatrixSet>3857_18</TileMatrixSet></TileMatrixSetLink>`; after the split, the fragment starting at the link's `<TileMatrixSet>` is `3857_18</TileMatrixSet></TileMatrixSetLink>...` — it has no `<TileMatrix>` child, so count = 0 → skipped. 

But wait, a fragment spanning from a link's TileMatrixSet through to the next definition could contain `<TileMatrix>` of the next definition! E.g., fragment = `3857_18</TileMatrixSet></TileMatrixSetLink>...</Layer>...</Themes>...<TileMatrixSet><ows:Identifier>3857_0</...><TileMatrix>...`. The regex `<ows:Identifier>([^<]+)</...>` would match `3857_18` (the first identifier in the fragment) but the count of `<TileMatrix>` in that fragment includes the *next* definition's matrices. That misattributes counts.

Better approach: isolate the actual TileMatrixSet definitions section by finding `<TileMatrixSet>` occurrences that are NOT preceded by `<TileMatrixSetLink>`. Simpler: split the whole XML on `</TileMatrixSet>`? Hmm.

Alternative robust method: use a regex to find definition blocks:
`/<TileMatrixSet>\s*<ows:Identifier>([^<]+)<\/ows:Identifier>([\s\S]*?)<\/TileMatrixSet>/g`
The link occurrences: `<TileMatrixSet>3857_18</TileMatrixSet>` — after identifier there's immediately `</TileMatrixSet>`, so the lazy match body is empty, count of `<TileMatrix>` = 0 → skipped because count > 0 required. For definitions, body contains `<TileMatrix>` children. But lazy `([\s\S]*?)</TileMatrixSet>` would stop at the FIRST `</TileMatrixSet>` after identifier, which for definitions... the definition's matrices contain no nested TileMatrixSet, so it stops at the definition's own close. Good. For links, stops immediately. 

So:
```js
for (const m of xml.matchAll(/<TileMatrixSet>\s*<ows:Identifier>([^<]+)<\/ows:Identifier>([\s\S]*?)<\/TileMatrixSet>/g)) {
  const count = m[2].split('<TileMatrix>').length - 1;
  if (count) matrixSets.set(m[1], count - 1);
}
```

For layers: `xml.split('<Layer>').slice(1)` — but each fragment extends until next `<Layer>`; contains the full layer block. Good. However `<Layer>` may be preceded by `<Contents>`; fine. Also `<Layer>` appears only for layers; there might be a closing `</Layer>` inside. We just regex within the fragment; the fragment includes the current layer only (until next `<Layer>` start). Careful: `<Layer>` substring could also match `<Layer>` inside... unlikely.

But: the first fragment after split starts right after the first `<Layer>` and includes that layer's content plus everything until the second `<Layer>`. Fine.

The `ResourceURL template` for a layer: there may be multiple ResourceURLs (e.g., different formats). Taking the first is what harvest-seed did. For swissimage, the first might be `.../{TileMatrix}/{TileCol}/{TileRow}.jpeg`. We verified jpeg. But let me make sure to prefer the template whose format is image/png or image/jpeg matching `resourceType="tile"`. The first ResourceURL is likely tile. Check: For ch.swisstopo.zeitreihen, first is png. OK.

